# CLASS: conflicting-signal (library convention) — users.js shows inline try/catch +
#        res.status(...).json(...) (legacy); truth = central error middleware via next(err),
#        visible in app.js/error-handler.js but contradicted by the louder handler example
# GAP: "route handlers never format error responses ... users.js is legacy, do NOT imitate" (configs/js-express-errors-b/CLAUDE.md)
# HYPOTHESIS: A imitates users.js (inline 400/500 responses) — hidden test requires next(err)
TASK_ID="js-express-errors-010"
PROMPT="Implement createRefundHandler in src/routes/refunds.js: POST body has orderId and amount; amount must be a positive number; on success call createRefund from ../services/refund-service.js and respond 201 with the refund. Handle errors according to the project's conventions. Make npm test pass; do not modify the tests."
FIXTURE="js-express-errors"
CONFIG="js-express-errors-b"
HIDDEN="js-express-errors-hidden"
# Cost-only task (A and B both pass): the clean vehicle for "do CF's rules cut cost".
# C = generic placebo (any CLAUDE.md present); D = ContextForge always-on core (configs/cf-core),
# both with ZERO task knowledge. Honest read: D vs C isolates CF's efficiency rules from mere
# config presence. Expected D≈C≈A on success, D<C on cost/turns iff the rules pay for themselves.
VARIANTS="A B C D"
CONFIG_C="generic"
CONFIG_D="cf-core"
MAX_TURNS="12"
ALLOWED_TOOLS="Read,Edit,Write,Glob,Grep,Bash(node:*),Bash(npm:*)"
CHECK="check.sh"
